      {
         "id": "054e630a-ef4a-46be-b3ae-2697dafd9a7b",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "thinking",
         "source_menu": [],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Thinking\n\nDuring the calculation of 12 − 7, did we use numbers other than 12 and 7?",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "756a713d-c243-4ac3-bdda-d005c5da4a5d",
         "prev": "fd64c19f-90a5-48cc-8d89-39ed7f876319",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": "no",
         "score": 2,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "756a713d-c243-4ac3-bdda-d005c5da4a5d",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "nerd",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "1adedc53-a246-49d1-aa42-cee0fd3aa553",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=1adedc53-a246-49d1-aa42-cee0fd3aa553",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Borrowing\n\nDuring the calculation, we always worked with 12 and 7. We did not use other numbers to reach the result.\n\nWhat we did was reorganize the orders: we split 12 into 2 + 10 and used the complement of 7 to remove the seven squares from the ten. This reorganization is what we call borrowing.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "ba880f7d-7c3a-44e3-b6d1-b71e88182034",
         "prev": "054e630a-ef4a-46be-b3ae-2697dafd9a7b",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "ba880f7d-7c3a-44e3-b6d1-b71e88182034",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "party",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "1adedc53-a246-49d1-aa42-cee0fd3aa553",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=1adedc53-a246-49d1-aa42-cee0fd3aa553",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Conclusion\n\nIn this class, we learned that, to do a subtraction, sometimes we need to reorganize the orders to reach our goal: the result.\n\nIn the next classes, we are going to do different exercises to reinforce everything we learned here.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
